---
title: Periods and year-end closing
description: Fiscal years in nullish, and how to close one.
---

A period usually covers one fiscal year. Entries can only be booked in an open period. Manage them under **Settings → Accounting → Periods**.

## Periods

nullish creates the current calendar year when you create your organization. To add the next one, choose **Create a period**: the dates are prefilled for the following year.

Your fiscal year doesn't follow the calendar, or your first year is shorter or longer? Set the **Start date** and **End date** freely. Periods just can't overlap.

## Close a year

Closing a year books its result and carries your balances over to the next year.

{% steps %}

1. Close the year's [VAT periods](/en/accounting/vat/#vat-periods).

2. Make sure the next period exists. Create it if needed.

3. On the year's period, choose **Close**.

{% /steps %}

nullish then books two entries:

- **Annual result**, on the last day of the year: the profit or loss is moved to your equity.
- **Opening balance**, on the first day of the next year: the balances of your assets, liabilities and equity are carried over.

For a sole proprietorship, the private account and capital contributions and withdrawals are also brought together on the capital account.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Once closed, nothing can be booked or changed in that year anymore. There's no reopening, so check
your balance sheet first.
{% /aside %}

## Only mark it as closed

When the closing entries are already in your books, for example after a [migration](/en/migration/bexio/), tick **Only mark it as closed**. nullish locks the year without booking anything.

Years have to be closed in order, the oldest first.
